Most poo rots away within days or is eaten by insects. To fossilise, it has to be buried quickly, in mud or sand, before it falls apart. Over time, water carries minerals into it, and they slowly replace the original material, just as with [[@fossils-form|bones]].

Meat-eaters' poo fossilises more often than plant-eaters'. It is rich in calcium phosphate from the bones they ate, which helps it harden. Plant-eaters' poo was softer and more often broke up before it could be buried. Some coprolites are found far from any skeleton, so working out which animal made them takes detective work too.

{{story:stomach-digest}}

कुछ सबसे अच्छे सुराग [[fossil|जीवाश्म]] हैं। [[@ammonites|अमोनाइट]] कुंडलीदार खोल वाले समुद्री जीव थे, जो आज के ऑक्टोपस और स्क्विड के रिश्तेदार थे। तो जब हिमालय की ऊँचाइयों पर, जैसे स्पीति के लांगज़ा गाँव में, अमोनाइट मिलते हैं, तो यह सबूत है कि वे चट्टानें कभी किसी समुद्र की तली थीं। जब भारत एशिया से टकराया, तो वह समुद्र दबकर ऊपर उठ गया।

[[@coral-bleaching|मूँगा]] भी इसी तरह काम आता है। मूँगे सिर्फ़ गरम, साफ़, उथले समुद्रों में उगते हैं। किसी ठंडे देश में जीवाश्म मूँगे की पट्टी का मतलब है कि वह ज़मीन कभी [[tropical|उष्णकटिबंधीय]] समुद्र थी।

{{story:coral-reefs}}

The temperature at which water boils or freezes depends on pressure. A [[@pressure-cooker|pressure cooker]] traps steam, raising the pressure so water stays liquid above 100 °C. That's why dal cooks faster in it.

Freezing works the same way in reverse. At pressures thousands of times greater than our air, the freezing point climbs above room temperature. At even higher pressures, ice VII can stay solid at several hundred degrees, hotter than a pizza oven.

{{story:pressure-cooker}}

How does a bird keep up in a flock of thousands? In 2008, Italian scientists filmed starling flocks over Rome with several cameras at once. They used computers to work out the position of every bird in 3D.

They found that each starling paid attention to about six or seven neighbours, not to everyone nearby. It didn't matter if the flock was packed tight or spread out. The number stayed about the same. This makes the flock stable, even when it stretches thin.

{{story:algorithms}}

In 1794, the English chemist John Dalton described his own odd colour vision to a society of scientists in Manchester. His paper was printed in 1798. Things others called red looked to him much like green. He had what we now call colour blindness, and for a while it was even called "Daltonism".

About 1 in 12 men of European ancestry, and fewer in many other groups, have red-green colour blindness. It's much rarer in women, because the [[gene|genes]] for red and green cones sit on the X [[chromosome|chromosome]]. Women have two X chromosomes, so a working copy on one usually makes up for the other.

{{story:tiger-stripes}}

पौधों से बने रंग सूती कपड़े से धुलकर निकल जाते हैं, जब तक कोई चीज़ उन्हें थामे न रखे। इस साथी को [[mordant|रंगबंधक]] कहते हैं। कलमकारी में कपड़े को पहले हरड़ के फल और दूध से बने घोल में भिगोया जाता है। फिर जहाँ-जहाँ लाल रंग चाहिए, वहाँ फिटकरी लगाई जाती है। जब कपड़े को लाल रंग देने वाली एक जड़ के साथ उबाला जाता है, तो रंग सिर्फ़ वहीं चिपकता है जहाँ फिटकरी लगी हो।

नीला रंग नील से आता है, पीला फूलों और फलों के छिलकों से, और हरा, नीले के ऊपर पीला चढ़ाकर। हर चरण के बीच कपड़े को बहते पानी में धोकर धूप में सुखाया जाता है। एक कपड़ा दर्जन भर से भी ज़्यादा चरणों से गुज़र सकता है और इसमें कई हफ़्ते लग जाते हैं।

{{story:east-india-company}}

1886 में वे थियो के साथ रहने [[@paris|पेरिस]] चले गए। वहाँ उन्होंने [[@impressionism|इम्प्रेशनिस्ट]] और दूसरे नए चित्रकारों का काम देखा, और उनके रंग खिल उठे। उन्होंने जापानी लकड़ी के ठप्पों वाले छापे भी इकट्ठा किए, और उनके चटक [[pigment|रंगों]] के सपाट हिस्से और गहरी रेखाएँ उन्हें बहुत भाईं।

वे रंग-चक्र पर एक-दूसरे के सामने पड़ने वाले रंगों को, जैसे नीला और नारंगी, अगल-बगल रखने लगे। ऐसी जोड़ियाँ एक-दूसरे को और चटक दिखाती हैं। रंग-चक्र रोशनी के [[spectrum|वर्णक्रम]] के रंगों को एक गोले में सजाकर बनता है।

{{story:night-sky-dark}}

In 1951, most Indians could not read or write. Experts around the world wondered whether a fair election was even possible. The answer was symbols. Every party got a simple picture, such as a pair of bullocks, a lamp or a hand, so voters could pick one without reading a name.

The first general election lasted from October 1951 to February 1952. About 17 crore people were eligible, and voting went smoothly enough to impress observers abroad. Symbols are still used today, on machines instead of paper.

{{story:democracy-athens}}

[[@helicopters|Helicopters]] have changed mountain rescue. A flight of 20 minutes can replace a two-day carry. But high up, the air is thin, so [[rotor|rotor]] blades have less air to push against. Above about 6,000 m, only special helicopters flown by very skilled pilots can manage.

In 2013 a pilot rescued a climber on Everest at about 7,800 m. He hung the climber from a long rope below the helicopter instead of trying to land. In India, the 2013 floods around Kedarnath trapped huge numbers of pilgrims. The [[@indian-air-force|Indian Air Force]] and army flew rescue missions for days, and tens of thousands of people were evacuated.

{{story:helicopters}}

In 1999, psychologists Daniel Simons and Christopher Chabris at Harvard showed people a short video of students passing basketballs. Viewers had to count the passes. Halfway through, a person in a gorilla suit walked into the middle, thumped their chest and walked out.

About half the viewers did not see the gorilla at all. Scientists call this inattentional blindness. When we focus hard on one task, we can miss something obvious. Magicians use the same weakness, giving you something to watch while the real work happens elsewhere.

{{story:eyes-see}}

In 1927, the Indian Broadcasting Company began broadcasting from Bombay and Calcutta. In 1936 the service became [[@radio-india|All India Radio]], and in 1957 it took the name Akashvani, meaning "voice from the sky". The word had first been used in the 1930s for a small station in Mysore, and it fitted perfectly.

On the night of 14 August 1947, many Indians heard [[@nehru|Jawaharlal Nehru]] give his "Tryst with Destiny" speech, broadcast as India became independent at midnight. Radio also carried [[@pop-cricket-commentary|cricket commentary]], farm advice and classical music concerts to villages far from any stadium or concert hall.

{{story:radio-waves}}

When you are nervous, your [[@heart-beat|heart beats]] faster and your hands shake a little. In shooting, that tiny shake can ruin a score. So shooters train their minds as much as their bodies.

Many use breathing exercises, meditation and visualisation, picturing a perfect shot before making it. Top shooters often fire in the brief pause after breathing out. Some even learn to feel their pulse and shoot between beats, which is a kind of extreme [[@mindfulness|mindfulness]].

{{story:heart-beat}}

धूल के कीट पानी नहीं पीते। वे इसे अपने शरीर से सीधे हवा से सोख लेते हैं। इसीलिए इन्हें मुंबई, चेन्नई, कोलकाता और फ़्लोरिडा जैसी [[humidity|उमस]] भरी जगहें पसंद हैं, और इसीलिए पहाड़ों की सूखी हवा या रेगिस्तानी कस्बों में ये मुश्किल से टिकते हैं।

इन्हें रोशनी से भी नफ़रत है, इसलिए ये कपड़ों में गहरे घुस जाते हैं। इन्हें ठीक से देखने के लिए [[@microscopes|सूक्ष्मदर्शी]] चाहिए। उसमें ये टाँगों वाले गोल-मटोल, रोएँदार गोलों जैसे दिखते हैं, कुछ-कुछ नन्हे भालू की तरह।

{{story:microscopes}}

Apps also record behaviour. They note which posts you stop scrolling at, what you search for, what time you go to bed and which buttons you press. Each click seems tiny, but together they build a detailed picture of you.

Many apps share data with other companies, such as advertising networks and analytics firms. Some companies, called data brokers, buy information from many sources and combine it. This is how a shoe you looked at once can follow you around the internet for days. It isn't magic or spying by one app alone. It's many small pieces of data passed from company to company.

{{story:advertising}}

Before Hammurabi, Babylon was one small city-state among many. He spent decades fighting and making deals with his neighbours until he controlled most of southern Mesopotamia. Scribes wrote on clay tablets in wedge-shaped cuneiform script, and thousands of their letters, contracts and school exercises survive.

Babylonian scholars were very good at maths. They used a system based on 60 and solved problems we would now call algebra. One famous tablet, known as Plimpton 322, lists sets of numbers that fit the rule about right-angled triangles now named after Pythagoras. It was written more than a thousand years before he was born. Their [[@number-systems|counting in sixties]] is still on every clock.

{{story:time-measure}}

1940 से 1946 तक आज़ाद ने फिर से कांग्रेस की अगुवाई की। यह असामान्य रूप से लंबा कार्यकाल था, क्योंकि युद्ध और जेल की वजह से पार्टी नए चुनाव नहीं करा पाई। अगस्त 1942 में जब [[@quit-india|भारत छोड़ो]] आंदोलन शुरू हुआ, तो अंग्रेज़ों ने कांग्रेस के सारे बड़े नेताओं को गिरफ़्तार कर लिया। आज़ाद, [[@nehru|नेहरू]], सरदार पटेल और दूसरे नेता लगभग तीन साल महाराष्ट्र के अहमदनगर किले में बंद रहे।

वहाँ आज़ाद ने एक दोस्त के नाम ऐसी चिट्ठियाँ लिखीं जो भेजी नहीं जा सकती थीं। बाद में ये *ग़ुबार-ए-ख़ातिर* नाम की किताब बनीं। उनके जेल में रहते ही उनकी पत्नी ज़ुलैख़ा बीमार पड़ीं और 1943 में चल बसीं, और आज़ाद को उनसे मिलने तक नहीं दिया गया।

{{story:urdu}}

A good Roman road was built like a layered sandwich. Workers dug a trench down to firm ground. They filled it with large stones, then smaller stones and gravel, then sand or crushed stone set with lime. On top, they fitted big flat paving stones tightly together.

The surface curved slightly, higher in the middle and lower at the edges, so rainwater ran off into ditches on each side. Many roads were raised on a bank above the land around them, which kept them dry and gave soldiers a better view. Modern [[@roads-built|roads are built]] on the same idea of firm layers and good drainage.

{{story:roads-built}}

More than 1.5 lakh people live on Majuli, in well over a hundred villages. Many belong to the Mising community, who build their houses on bamboo stilts called *chang ghar* so that floodwater can pass underneath. Rice fields, fishing and handloom [[weaving|weaving]] are part of everyday life. Potters on the island shape clay by hand, without a wheel, in a style that is centuries old.

There is no bridge yet from the mainland. People cross by ferry, sometimes with bicycles and motorbikes balanced on the deck. In 2016 Majuli became a district of its own, the first island in India to be given that status.

{{story:northeast-india}}

[[@kolkata|कोलकाता]] में चीनी लोग लगभग 250 साल से रह रहे हैं। जिस पहले बाशिंदे का नाम आम तौर पर लिया जाता है, वे हैं तोंग आछू। कहा जाता है कि 1700 के दशक के आख़िर में उन्होंने हुगली नदी के पास चीनी (शक्कर) की एक मिल लगाई। वह इलाक़ा आज भी उन्हीं के नाम पर अछीपुर कहलाता है, और उनकी क़ब्र भी वहीं है।

अगली सदी में और लोग [[migration|प्रवास]] करके आए, जिनमें बहुत-से दक्षिणी चीन के हक्का और कैंटोनीज़ समुदायों से थे। वे बढ़ई, दाँतों के डॉक्टर, मोची और चमड़ा कमाने वाले बने। शहर में दो चाइनाटाउन बस गए, एक पुराने शहर में टेरेटी बाज़ार के आसपास, और दूसरा तांगरा में, चमड़े के कारख़ानों के बीच।

{{story:kolkata}}

Food is made of tiny [[cell|cells]], each a bag of watery juice. When water freezes slowly, it forms large, sharp ice crystals. They grow and pierce the cell walls. When the food thaws, the juice leaks out and the food becomes limp.

When water freezes fast, it has no time to build big crystals. Instead it forms millions of tiny ones, which leave most of the cell walls whole. Birdseye built a machine that pressed packed food between metal plates chilled to very low temperatures. In 1930, the first quick-frozen foods under his name went on sale in Springfield, Massachusetts.

{{story:refrigerator}}
